WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.082
- This video was brought
to you by Legal Eagle's

00:00:02.082 --> 00:00:04.830
CopyrightCourse.com, giving
all creators the tools

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.040
they need to fight bogus copyright claims.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:07.873
(eagle screams)

00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:09.950
YouTube stands up for creators everywhere

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.950
and fights back against
an alleged copyright troll

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.770
who not only apparently tried
to extort fellow YouTubers,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.350
but tried to have one killed.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.780
Yep, it may be true.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.730
This case is wild.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.230
(regal music)

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:27.433
Hey, Legal Eagles.

00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:29.650
It's time to think like all of the lawyers

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.710
that YouTube just hired to
go after a copyright troll

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.690
who may have been trying to extort a bunch

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.550
of individual creators.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.670
YouTube has filed a federal lawsuit

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.630
against a YouTube creator
who they claim was trying

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.420
to extort fellow creators

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.270
and actually may have swatted one of them.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:47.890
Now, before I begin I want to emphasize

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.790
that these are only allegations.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.890
All of these facts come from the complaint

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.960
that YouTube filed in federal court.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.430
A complaint is the document
that you use to start a lawsuit

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.870
and it contains your side of the story.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.450
YouTube is the plaintiff here.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.010
That means they started the lawsuit.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.880
And because we're still
in the very early stages

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.530
of this particular lawsuit,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.330
all of the facts are
gonna come from YouTube.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.220
So take everything with a grain of salt.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.790
Now first lemme give you some background

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.350
to help understand this
lawsuit that YouTube has filed.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.620
Congress passed the DMCA in 1998

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.300
to deal with copyright-infringing
material online.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.090
It allows websites to not be responsible

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.860
for copyright-infringing material

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.880
that its users upload under
certain circumstances.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.660
The DMCA is one of two laws

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.650
that give websites a safe harbor,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.580
the other being the
Communications Decency Act,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.410
Section 230, which is something

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:37.860
I will definitely cover in the future

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.800
because it is in the news
every single day these days.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.780
The bottom line is that, under the DMCA,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.620
a website isn't liable for
copyright-infringing material

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.140
so long as they take down
the offending material

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.650
when they receive a notice from the person

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.000
that owns the copyright.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.340
This is called a DMCA takedown notice.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.990
You'll find it in 17 USC 512,

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.570
which is a totally easy law to understand.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.060
You can look it up by searching
Google for 17 USC 512.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.760
Now, YouTube asserts in its complaint

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.610
that one Christopher Brady issued

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:11.110
a false DMCA takedown notice
to extort certain YouTubers,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.500
people that made Minecraft videos.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.520
Basically the complaint alleges

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.300
that he claimed he owned the copyright

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.680
to Minecraft and therefore demanded

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.490
that YouTubers pay him money

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.430
or he would issue a DMCA
takedown, a third one.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:25.910
Now, YouTube has a system where

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.120
if you receive three DMCA takedown notices

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.940
your channel is deleted.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.310
When a DMCA takedown notice is
levied against your channel,

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:34.540
YouTube gives you a strike,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:38.320
and three strikes and you're
out of the YouTube game.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.130
Now, allegedly Brady would
issue two DMCA takedown notices

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.860
and then try to extort hundreds

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.000
of dollars from those creators

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.070
by threatening a third takedown notice,

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.730
which would result in
potentially the deletion

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.020
of these people's channel.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.420
Now, if true, this is illegal

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.420
and violates the DMCA Section 512.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.253
This happens.

00:02:58.253 --> 00:03:00.010
I've talked to creators who have dealt

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.010
with false DMCA takedowns.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.630
I, myself, have been the
victim of a false DMCA claim.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.810
So this is definitely something
that exists in the world.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.780
But it's also relatively unusual.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.250
The big boys, the music
labels and the movie studios,

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.220
rarely mess around with DMCA claims.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.190
Now, sure they abuse the content ID system

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:22.660
that YouTube has created,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.910
but a DMCA takedown notice
is a little bit different.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.560
It's created by copyright law

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.910
and YouTube doesn't have a
whole lot of control over it.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.230
Basically from the people that

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.130
I've talked to behind the scenes,

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.890
none of the big players
will file a DMCA takedown

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.390
without talking to a lawyer first,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:43.090
or often they'll have the
lawyer issue the DMCA takedown.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.140
Why is that?

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.440
Because it's a big (beeps) deal.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.490
As paragraph eight of
YouTube's complaint states,

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.680
to submit a takedown
notice using the form,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.460
parties must affirmatively check a box

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.767
next to a statement that reads,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.977
"I acknowledge that under Section 512 f

00:03:56.977 --> 00:03:58.657
"of the DMCA takedown, any person

00:03:58.657 --> 00:04:00.927
"who knowingly material misrepresents

00:04:00.927 --> 00:04:04.127
"that material or activity
is infringing may be subject

00:04:04.127 --> 00:04:06.020
"to liability for damages."

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.480
If you issue a false takedown,

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.850
not only are you liable for damages,

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.280
in other words the loss of revenue

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.140
that comes from the videos,

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.490
but you're also liable
for attorneys' fees,

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.880
and when you're talking about
YouTube, that's a lot of fees.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.870
Now, I know YouTube gets a lot of flak

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.730
for taking down videos pursuant

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.900
to the DMCA takedown request,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.930
but YouTube actually doesn't really have

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:31.270
much choice in the matter

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.190
that's apart from the
whole content ID system

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.620
which YouTube controls.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.700
Paragraph nine of YouTube's complaint says

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.020
that YouTube receives thousands

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.130
of DMCA takedown requests each week

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.350
through its online web form.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.170
It also received notices by email,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.820
postal mail, and facsimile.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:45.910
I'd guess that, if anything,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.660
this is probably an understatement.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.460
I'd guess that YouTube probably deals

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.070
with on the order of millions
of DMCA takedowns per year,

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.530
and YouTube doesn't really have

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.520
much choice in how to deal
with these particular claims.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.420
It's all enshrined in copyright law,

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.960
both the timeframes and the consequences.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.280
So if YouTube gets a DMCA takedown,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.520
they don't really have much choice

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.400
but to take the video off of YouTube,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.410
otherwise YouTube would potentially lose

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.000
their copyright safe harbor

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.810
and become liable for thousands,

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.370
if not hundreds of thousands,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.620
of copyright infringement
actions per year.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.100
So let's talk about the
actual facts of this case.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.980
In paragraph 21 of the complaint,

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.630
YouTube alleges that in January 2019,

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.500
defendant Brady, using
several falsified identities,

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:31.920
sent YouTube multiple notices

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.914
of alleged copyright infringement
pursuant to the DMCA,

00:05:34.914 --> 00:05:35.950
claiming that two videos uploaded

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.790
by Kenzo and two videos
uploaded by ObbyRaidz

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.150
supposedly infringed
copyrights that he owned.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.010
In the next paragraph, YouTube asserts

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:44.900
he represented that he
was the original creator

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.540
of those videos and that he
held the copyright to them.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.020
The complaint goes on to assert

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.534
that Brady tried to
extort a third YouTuber,

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:52.980
and in fighting one of those

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.310
allegedly false DMCA takedown notices,

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.130
that YouTuber provided
his true home address

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.870
as required by law.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:00.703
That makes sense.

00:06:00.703 --> 00:06:02.150
That is one of those
statutory requirements

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.120
to being able to fight
a DMCA takedown notice.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.100
The problem, as alleged in this complaint,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.180
is that part of the DMCA allows

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:12.100
you to issue a subpoena to get

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.740
that information when it's uploaded,

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.180
the purpose of which is that,

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:17.560
if you are issuing a DMCA takedown,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.120
you need to be able to take these people

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.220
to court if they file a counter-notice

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.960
and put the video back up.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.340
The complaint goes on to assert that

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:28.040
on July 10th the third
YouTuber announced via Twitter

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.440
that he'd been the victim of
a swatting scheme that day.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.430
And they go on to define swatting

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.100
as the act of making a bogus call

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.730
to emergency services in an attempt

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.270
to bring about the
dispatch of a large number

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.330
of armed police officers
to a particular address.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.750
If that's true that this
guy did use the information

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:48.580
he gained by abusing the
DMCA takedown process

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:53.030
to issue a false SWAT call
against a fellow YouTuber

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.700
in the hope that the
SWAT team would be called

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.030
to this guy's actual house,

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.240
that's a felony and this
guy should be in jail.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.980
This is a despicable act, if true.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.980
People have died because of swattings,

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.020
because if you're playing a video game

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.330
that involves gunfire and the
SWAT team hears the gunfire

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.790
from the outside, they might come

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.290
in with guns drawn and people have died

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.500
in the course of being falsely swatted.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.490
The bottom line is that YouTube alleges

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.050
that this particular individual,

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.160
Brady, falsified DMCA takedowns,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.790
committed perjury, and
used that information

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.060
that he gained to extort fellow YouTubers

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.650
and may have even tried to
kill one of those YouTubers.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.460
Frankly I was astounded when I saw

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.200
this lawsuit being filed by YouTube.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.560
17 USC 512 f does in fact
give service providers

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.550
like YouTube a private right of action

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:45.130
to sue over false DMCA takedown notices.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:47.710
I've just never seen a service provider

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.350
actually file a suit like this.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:51.240
If true, this seems like a very,

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.410
very good thing on
behalf of most YouTubers

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.990
and a blow against potential people

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.870
who are trying to abuse
the copyright process

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.880
for their own ends when
they don't own the copyright

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.260
to allegedly infringing material.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.300
This could be the first of many lawsuits

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.020
on behalf of YouTube
protecting its YouTubers,

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:15.020
and if indeed the allegations
in the complaint are true,

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:17.490
I think YouTube is doing all of us a favor

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:21.570
by getting rid of some of
these YouTube copyright trolls.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.590
I think YouTube is
trying to send a message

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:25.490
against this troll and potentially others.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.300
They have the resources to do it,

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.470
and I will repeat that we
don't know all of the facts.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.580
This is all one-sided, so please do

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.930
not dox the defendant, Brady,

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:37.340
or attack him in any way online.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.530
Let this legal process play out.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.570
YouTube, in its prayer for relief,

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:43.360
is asking for compensatory damages,

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.420
attorneys' fees, and an injunction,

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.610
in other words, a court order

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.030
that forces the defendant to stop.

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:51.190
This is probably bad enough.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.080
I checked and this case is being litigated

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:56.220
by a local firm in Omaha,
where the defendant lives,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.100
and also by a very well-known attorney

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.110
with one of the top-tier law firms,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.040
Wilson Sonsini, which is the law firm

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.020
that has handled Google and YouTube's

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.290
most important and famous cases.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.870
And I was curious how much
Wilson Sonsini charges,

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.590
and according to court documents,

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:14.770
if you can get a 10%
discount for a lead partner

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:19.200
at Wilson Sonsini, you
will only pay $900 an hour.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.750
In other words, if this defendant loses,

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:23.510
he'll probably pay on the order

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:27.990
of $1,000 per hour for hundreds
of hours of attorney time.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:29.930
Also a parenthetically
that Phil DeFranco covered

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:31.890
this lawsuit earlier today, and although

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.020
he did generally a great job covering it,

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.310
he put some special attention
to the end of the complaint,

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.240
specifically the prayer which asks

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.560
for such other further
and different relief

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.020
as the court deems proper
under the circumstances.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.760
- Kind of a question mark, saying that

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.540
they pray for such other, further,

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:49.380
and different relief as
the court deems proper

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.690
under the circumstances.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.090
So something even potentially bigger here.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:54.470
- He kinda freaked out about this.

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:56.740
That's a totally normal
thing to put in a complaint.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.680
It's just a catch-all, a lawyer's CYA,

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:00.960
to allow them to ask for something

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.720
else not specifically
mentioned in the complaint.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.680
So Phil, my dude, reach
out to me next time.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.070
I'll let you know what is notable

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:08.820
or not in a complaint like this.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.040
As an attorney I have
dealt with copyright trolls

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.420
for a very, very long time,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.380
and I am tired of creators getting pushed

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.430
around by bogus copyright claims.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:19.100
So I decided to do something about it.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.240
I made CopyrightCourse.com to give

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.920
all creators the tools they
need to fight meritless claims

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.100
and stand up for their rights online.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.400
It's not only an entire law school course

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.470
on copyright distilled into a few hours,

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.110
but it's also a system that I developed

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.040
to fight claims on YouTube
and other platforms.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.790
I should know because I get a lot

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:38.070
of claims against my videos,

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.100
even though they are clearly fair use,

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:43.810
and spoiler alert, made by
an actual copyright attorney

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:44.643
and trial lawyer.

